  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Onterumallikarjuna vs The State Of Andhra Pradesh on 22 March, 2022 Author: D.Ramesh Bench: D.Ramesh The Honourable Sri Justice D.Ramesh Criminal Petition No.6085 of 2019 O R D E R:",
    "facts": "",
    "arguments": "3. Learned counsel for the petitioners while denying the allegations made in the charge sheet, primarily assails the validity of the criminal prosecution launched against the petitioners under Section 188 IPC, on the ground that the bar engrafted under Section 195 Cr.P.C. clearly attracts to the present facts of the case, as there is no such complaint filed by the concerned authority, who had issued the initial order, said to be violated, or a person, who is administratively superior to the officer who issued the order. He further submits that even the allegations made in the FIR and charge sheet does not bring out the commission of any other offences alleged under sections 9(1) of A.P. Gaming Act, Section 272, 273, 328 of IPC and Section 57, 58 read with 26, 30 of FSS Act, 2006, since the tobacco products would not fall within the definition of Section 3(j) of FSS Act. 4. Learned Additional Public Prosecutor fairly concedes that except the offences under Gaming Act, other offences were covered in the earlier orders of this Court, thereby prayed to pass appropriate order in this Criminal Petition.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "3. Learned counsel for the petitioners while denying the allegations made in the charge sheet, primarily assails the validity of the criminal prosecution launched against the petitioners under Section 188 IPC, on the ground that the bar engrafted under Section 195 Cr.P.C. clearly attracts to the present facts of the case, as there is no such complaint filed by the concerned authority, who had issued the initial order, said to be violated, or a person, who is administratively superior to the officer who issued the order. He further submits that even the allegations made in the FIR and charge sheet does not bring out the commission of any other offences alleged under sections 9(1) of A.P. Gaming Act, Section 272, 273, 328 of IPC and Section 57, 58 read with 26, 30 of FSS Act, 2006, since the tobacco products would not fall within the definition of Section 3(j) of FSS Act.",
    "respondent_arguments": "4. Learned Additional Public Prosecutor fairly concedes that except the offences under Gaming Act, other offences were covered in the earlier orders of this Court, thereby prayed to pass appropriate order in this Criminal Petition.",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": ""
  },
